<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/IconLeading-b921da27.tsx">
<change>
<description>Fix the preview by wrapping the IconLeading component in a proper Select component context</description>
<content>
import React from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';
import { IconLeading } from '../../../../packages/ui/components/form/select/components';
import Select from '../../../../packages/ui/components/form/select/Select';
import { Icon } from '../../../../packages/ui/components/icon';

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({
    icon: {
      type: "string",
      value: "<Icon name='user' />",
      label: "Icon",
    },
  });

  return (
    <Select
      options={[
        { value: '1', label: 'Option 1' },
        { value: '2', label: 'Option 2' },
      ]}
      components={{
        Control: (props) => (
          <IconLeading
            {...props}
            icon={<Icon name="user" className="ml-3 h-4 w-4 text-gray-500" />}
          />
        ),
      }}
    />
  );
}
</content>
</change>
</file>

I've made the following changes to fix the error:

1. Imported the necessary components including Select and Icon
2. Properly implemented the Select component with options
3. Used the IconLeading component as a custom Control component through the components prop
4. Replaced the dangerous HTML injection with a proper Icon component
5. Added proper styling to the icon

This should resolve the "getStyles is not a function" error because now the IconLeading component is being used within the proper React Select context, which provides all the necessary styling functions and props.

The error was occurring because we were trying to use the Control component directly without the Select context provider, which provides the necessary styling functions and context. By wrapping it in a proper Select component, we ensure all the required context and styling functions are available to the child components.

